we faced a challenge like this the last  time sixty to seventy years ago at the  end of the Second World War when the  European international system that had  been dominant that time had been  devastated at the end of the at the end  of the war and we had visionary leaders  like Roosevelt like Truman like Dean  Atkinson in the United States Winston  Churchill in in England cotton Konrad  Adenauer  in Germany John mone in France and  understood that they needed to create a  set of institutions that would be  conducive toward stability and  prosperity so we got the United Nations  we've got the World Bank the  International Monetary Fund the European  Union to be at least the beginnings of  that and NATO a set of institutions that  actually led to a long period of peace  and prosperity and justice at least in  that part of the world not dominated by  communist systems and beyond that a  system that was resilient enough to  stand up to the challenges posed by a  hostile Soviet force the question now is  whether you will have the type of  visionary leadership to take advantage  of the opportunity now to build that  type of World Order now we're doing it  now when the challenges are evident but  at a time when we haven't gone through  30 years of war and economic collapse  the way those visionary leaders did 60  to 70 years ago and the question is can  we draw the lessons of the changes in a  sense preemptively or do we have to  awake for the for the real challenges  the real disasters to happen before we  see the wisdom of operating in a  different way well there have been some  efforts to what to deal with the changes  we've seen a debate on how the UN  Security Council is structured we've  seen some changes in the way the World  Bank and the IMF allocate voting rights  within their system but all of this has  really been too little to deal with the  dimensions of the challenges we're  facing so the real question for the next  decade is whether those new leaders will  